# Veripsa release gate (auto-discovered). One gate per file -> two PRs adding gates add two FILES, never a
# shared line -> no false merge conflict on registration. register_gate is defined in run_gates.sh.
# Format: register_gate <test> <marker> <name> <ok_blurb> <desc> [tail]
register_gate "tests/test_cochange_async.py" "CO-CHANGE OFF-WORKER GATE: PASS" "co-change populate is OFF the event worker (advisory clone must not stall the pipeline)" "CO-CHANGE OFF-WORKER: co-change is an ADVISORY second signal whose feeder is a git clone (I/O-bound, up to history_clone's timeout). Running it inline on the SINGLE webhook worker would stall every queued PR/push behind a slow clone AND hold the per-(account,repo) advisory lock for the clone's whole duration (a same-repo event would lock-timeout -> fail -> redeliver). So backfill_repo hands population to a DEDICATED single-thread pool (populate_cochange_async) and returns immediately; the pool clones/extracts holding NO lock (the slow part), then BRIEFLY tenant-pins + repo-locks to store -- copying the proven self_heal_main_graph off-event-path pattern. Proven against a real scratch-tenant DB: (1) NON-BLOCKING (populate_cochange_async returns immediately -- 0.01s -- even with a 2s clone, so the event worker is freed); (2) it still POPULATES in the RIGHT tenant (the pool task derives the owning account from gh.installation_account_id() and pins it -> editing auth surfaces api); (3) CROSS-TENANT ISOLATION holds through the async pin (a 2nd tenant sees NOTHING); (4) FAIL CLOSED (an unresolved owning account writes NOTHING -- never an unrouted/cross-tenant write); (5) CONTENT-FREE. The lock is held ONLY around the quick store, never the clone, so the pool thread never holds a repo lock while a slow clone runs" "co-change off-worker: populate_cochange_async returns immediately (clone never stalls the single event worker or holds the repo lock), still populates the RIGHT tenant, cross-tenant isolation + fail-closed-on-no-account preserved, content-free" 30
